      {
        "file": "a22_listening_sydney.mp3",
        "voice": "arthur",
        "text": "Hi Murielle, it's Daniel from the Sydney office again. Thanks for the call last week. You asked me how our HR team used to work before the merger, so here is a quick answer. Ten years ago, we used to have four separate offices, and each office used to run its own payroll. Managers didn't use to share data, so nobody had the full picture. Recruitment used to take about three months. Nowadays, we have one HR system for the whole country, and we hire in about five weeks. People used to complain about the paperwork, but now most of it is digital. One thing hasn't changed: we still meet face to face every Friday morning. Speak soon."
      },
      {
        "file": "a22_listening_reviews.mp3",
        "voice": "ellen",
        "text": "When I started my career in HR, performance reviews used to happen once a year. Managers used to fill in a long form, and employees used to wait twelve months for any feedback. Most people hated it. Nowadays, many companies use short check-ins every month instead. Managers didn't use to talk about wellbeing, but today it is part of almost every conversation. Some things should stay the same, though. Good managers always listened carefully, and that is still the most important skill. So my advice is simple: keep what worked, and replace what is outdated."
      }
